# Appendix: physical forcing of the columns

The biological model evolves inside a prescribed physical environment.
Light controls photosynthesis, and vertical mixing transports nutrients and
plankton through the water column. This appendix makes those inputs
inspectable alongside the biological experiments.

## Current reference column: idealised seasonal forcing

The reference simulation uses a 500 m column with 50 uniform cells,
a 30-minute time step and vertically implicit mixing. Its
annual forcing begins in midwinter and repeats every 365 days. The
reference tutorial spans the complete annual cycle, including spring, summer,
autumn and winter.

WORKSHOP_PHYSICS_FIGURE

The figure shows three related physical inputs:

- **Surface PAR:** An idealised annual cycle, with a winter minimum and
  stronger illumination toward midsummer. The prescribed value is in W m⁻².
- **Mixed-layer depth:** A diagnostic transition from a roughly 140 m winter
  mixed layer toward a roughly 15 m summer layer.
- **Vertical diffusivity:** Stronger mixing above the mixed-layer base and
  weaker mixing below it. The white curve marks the prescribed mixed-layer
  depth in the time-depth diagram.

These are the same functions imported from `MASTSWorkshop` in the reference
exercise: `surface_PAR(t)`, `mixed_layer_depth(t)` and
`vertical_diffusivity(z, t)`. The water column uses the prescribed
vertical diffusivity as its closure, and LOBSTER calculates the attenuation
of PAR within the column.

## Idealised BATS and NABE comparison

The [BATS-NABE comparison](07_bats_nabe.md) adds a North Atlantic spring-bloom
scenario and uses the same stretched grid at both stations. The original BATS
reference exercises keep their tested 500 m / 50-cell configuration.

| Setting | Deep BATS | Idealised NABE |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Location represented | 31.7 N, 64.2 W | ~47 N, 20 W |
| Winter mixed-layer depth | 140 m | ~350 m |
| Summer mixed-layer depth | 15 m | ~20 m |
| Surface PAR | 2-122 W/m2 | 2-98 W/m2 |
| Spring mixed-layer transition | Smooth annual cycle | Rapid shoaling around day 100 |
| Initial near-surface nitrate | ~0.7 mmol N/m3 | ~6.0 mmol N/m3 |
| Vertical diffusivity, surface mixed layer | up to ~5.1e-3 m2/s | up to ~1.01e-2 m2/s |

These parameters are **first-pass teaching scenarios**, chosen to distinguish
subtropical conditions and a bloom-forming temperate Atlantic environment.
The nitrate profiles are depth-dependent but are not yet calibrated against
station observations. Initial plankton and organic-matter fields also decrease
with depth to limit artificial deep biological inventories. The annual cycle begins in midwinter.

The stretched domain reaches 1200 m with 60 cells, preserving 10 m resolution
through the upper 300 m, then increasing to 20 m and 40 m cells below 300 m
and 600 m. Export can be diagnosed at grid faces 100, 300, and 1000 m. All
vertical inventories use the physical thickness of each cell.

### Scientific sources and next validation

- [OceanBioME idealised North Atlantic column](https://oceanbiome.github.io/OceanBioME.jl/stable/generated/column/):
  precedent for deep winter mixing, rapid spring shoaling and seasonal PAR.
- [NOAA World Ocean Atlas 2023](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/world-ocean-atlas-2023/):
  source for the planned station-specific nitrate and temperature climatologies.
- [U.S. JGOFS NABE field observations](https://www.bco-dmo.org/dataset/2574/description):
  trace-metal and water-column measurements from the 1989 field campaign.

The present LOBSTER column is forced by prescribed light and vertical mixing;
temperature is not passed as a separate prognostic tracer or remineralisation
forcing. Temperature-dependent remineralisation is a candidate follow-up
experiment once the default column behaviour has been benchmarked.

Return to the [reference column](00_reference_column.md) or continue with the
[grazing experiment](01_grazing.md).
